<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Reranking is a critical component in many information retrieval pipelines. Despite remarkable progress in text-only settings, multimodal reranking remains challenging, particularly when the candidate set contains <em id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">hybrid</em> text and image items. A key difficulty is the modality gap: a text reranker is intrinsically closer to text candidates than to image candidates, leading to biased and suboptimal cross-modal ranking. Vision-language models (VLMs) mitigate this gap through strong cross-modal alignment and have recently been adopted to build multimodal rerankers. However, most VLM-based rerankers encode <em id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">all</em> candidates as images, and treating text as images introduces substantial computational overhead. Meanwhile, existing open-source multimodal rerankers are typically trained on general-domain data and often underperform in domain-specific scenarios.
To address these limitations, we propose <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">UniRank</span>, a VLM-based reranking framework that natively scores and orders <em id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">hybrid</em> text-image candidates without any modality conversion. Building on this hybrid scoring interface, <span id="abstract1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">UniRank</span> provides an end-to-end domain adaptation pipeline that includes:

<span id="S0.I1" class="ltx_inline-enumerate">
<span id="S0.I1.i1" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item">(1)</span> <span id="S0.I1.i1.2" class="ltx_text">an instruction-tuning stage that learns calibrated cross-modal relevance scoring by mapping label-token likelihoods to a unified scalar score; and
</span></span>
<span id="S0.I1.i2" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item">(2)</span> <span id="S0.I1.i2.2" class="ltx_text">a hard-negative-driven preference alignment stage that constructs in-domain pairwise preferences and performs query-level policy optimization through reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF).
</span></span>
</span>
Extensive experiments on scientific literature retrieval and design patent search demonstrate that <span id="abstract1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">UniRank</span> consistently outperforms state-of-the-art baselines, improving Recall@1 by 8.9% and 7.3%, respectively.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>Multimodal Reranking, Vision-Language Models, Domain-Specific Fine-Tuning, Hybrid Text-Image Candidates
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Information retrieval (IR) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Baeza-Yates et al., 1999</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Manning, 2008</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chowdhury, 2010</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hambarde and Proenca, 2023</a>)</cite> aims to identify and return the most relevant items from a large corpus in response to a user query. Modern IR systems are commonly built as a multi-stage pipeline, where <em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">embedding-based retrieval</em> and <em id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">reranking</em> serve as two fundamental components <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. In the first stage, a dense embedding model maps queries and items into a shared vector space, enabling efficient approximate nearest neighbor search to retrieve a small set of potentially relevant candidates from millions of documents. In the second stage, a reranker performs more fine-grained relevance modeling over the candidate set and produces the final ranked list, ensuring that the most relevant results are placed at the top. This retrieve-then-rerank paradigm has delivered substantial improvements in text-only IR and has become a standard practice in many real-world applications <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lewis et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Edge et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fan et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the past few years, the demand for multimodal retrieval has rapidly grown <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mei et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, driven by applications such as e-commerce search <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendriksen, 2022</a>)</cite>, scientific literature discovery <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and content recommendation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yue et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, where both text and images may serve as queries and/or candidates. However, directly extending text rerankers to such settings remains challenging due to the <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">modality gap</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geigle et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>: these models are inherently biased toward textual inputs and struggle to fairly compare relevance across heterogeneous modalities. A common workaround is to first convert images into textual descriptions (e.g., captions or VLM-derived text) and then apply a text reranker. While feasible, this approach is fundamentally limited because textual descriptions cannot always faithfully capture the rich and fine-grained semantics of images, leading to a low performance ceiling in practice <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Giouroukis et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dong et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. These limitations motivate the need for dedicated multimodal rerankers that can reason over both visual and textual content.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Benefiting from recent advances in VLMs and their strong cross-modal alignment capability, an emerging research direction is to build multimodal rerankers on top of VLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. Nevertheless, existing VLM-based rerankers still have notable limitations. First, many approaches homogenize the input format by treating <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">all</em> candidates as images so that the reranker can operate in a single visual input space. This design simplifies modeling but incurs substantial storage and inference overhead, and becomes particularly inefficient when the candidate pool is dominated by textual items or requires cross-modal reranking between text and images. Second, while several open-source multimodal rerankers perform well on general-domain benchmarks, they are typically trained on broad web-scale data and lack specialization for domain-specific content. As a result, their performance often degrades when deployed in contexts such as e-commerce, finance, or defense, where private data and unique semantics dominate <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Crucially, there remains a lack of comprehensive <em id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">end-to-end training frameworks</em> tailored for building domain-adaptive multimodal rerankers—a gap that limits the deployment of high-performance retrieval systems in privacy-sensitive and application-critical scenarios.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To this end, we propose <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">UniRank</span>, an end-to-end framework for training domain-specific rerankers that directly operate on hybrid text-image candidates without modality conversion. Starting from a pretrained VLM backbone, we first construct high-quality instruction-following data and perform supervised fine-tuning (SFT) to obtain a VLM-based reranker that produces meaningful and comparable relevance scores for hybrid candidates. We then run the SFT reranker on held-out in-domain data to mine <em id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">hard negatives</em>—high-scoring but non-relevant candidates—and pair them with gold positives to build large-scale pairwise preference data. Finally, we leverage this preference dataset to train a reward model (RM) and further optimize the reranker via RLHF, improving both score calibration and ranking robustness in domain-specific settings. Our main contributions are summarized as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hybrid-modality VLM reranking.</span> We introduce <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">UniRank</span>, a VLM-based multimodal reranking framework that natively operates on <em id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">hybrid</em> candidate sets containing both text and images, enabling unified and fair cross-modal relevance scoring without converting text into images, thus reducing storage and inference overhead.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">End-to-end domain-specific alignment framework.</span> We propose an end-to-end, two-stage training framework for domain-specific multimodal rerankers, including

<span id="S1.I1.i2.I1" class="ltx_inline-enumerate">
<span id="S1.I1.i2.I1.i1" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item">(1)</span> <span id="S1.I1.i2.I1.i1.2" class="ltx_text">instruction-driven SFT to learn calibrated cross-modal relevance scoring, and
</span></span>
<span id="S1.I1.i2.I1.i2" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item">(2)</span> <span id="S1.I1.i2.I1.i2.2" class="ltx_text">hard-negative mining followed by RLHF to construct in-domain pairwise preferences for reward modeling and policy optimization.</span></span>
</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Strong empirical results on two real-world domains.</span> We evaluate on scientific literature retrieval and design patent search, and show that <span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">UniRank</span> consistently outperforms state-of-the-art multimodal rerankers, achieving up to 8.9% and 7.3% gains in Recall@1, respectively.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Rerankers</h3>

<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Text rerankers</h5>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Text reranking has been extensively studied under the retrieve-then-rerank paradigm, where the reranker refines a candidate set returned by a fast first-stage retriever. A dominant line of work adopts the <em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cross-encoder</em> architecture, which jointly encodes the query and a candidate document by concatenation and leverages Transformer-based full token-level interactions to estimate relevance, as in bge-rerankers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiao et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, jina-rerankers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and mxbai-rerankers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shakir et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Another representative direction is <em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">late interaction</em>, exemplified by ColBERT-style models, which encode queries and documents independently and perform efficient token-level matching at inference time <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Khattab and Zaharia, 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023a</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Multimodal rerankers</h5>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Multimodal reranking extends the above problem to settings where queries and/or candidates may contain images in addition to text, and the candidate pool can be heterogeneous (e.g., mixed text and image items) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2012</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mortaheb et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Existing approaches can be broadly categorized into two groups. The first group reduces multimodality to text by converting images into textual representations, such as captions and VLM-extracted text, and then applying a text reranker <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dong et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. While simple and compatible with mature text rerankers, this strategy may lose visual details and can be bottlenecked by the quality of the intermediate descriptions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Giouroukis et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. The second group builds rerankers directly on top of VLMs, leveraging their cross-modal alignment to compare relevance between queries and multimodal candidates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Faysse et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Recent examples include VLM-based rerankers enhanced with chain-of-thought prompting and task-specific fine-tuning for page-level reranking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, as well as methods that finetune Qwen3-VL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> to obtain a reranker by framing reranking as a binary classification problem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. Different from these prior efforts, <span id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">UniRank</span> targets <em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cross-modal</em> reranking over <em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">hybrid</em> text-image candidate sets while emphasizing an end-to-end training framework for <em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">domain-specific</em> adaptation.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Instruction Tuning and Preference Alignment</h3>

<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Supervised fine-tuning</h5>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Supervised fine-tuning adapts a pretrained language or vision–language model to downstream tasks by training on curated instruction–response pairs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. In the post-instruction-tuning era, SFT has become a standard recipe to elicit desired behaviors such as following task specifications, producing structured outputs, and learning task-specific scoring or classification heads. To reduce training cost and memory footprint, parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) methods are widely used <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ding et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, such as LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, QLora <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dettmers et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and AdaLoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. These techniques enable fast customization of large models while keeping most backbone parameters frozen, making them particularly appealing for domain adaptation with limited compute or privacy constraints.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Preference alignment and RLHF</h5>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond SFT, reinforcement learning from human feedback is commonly employed to align model outputs with human or application-specific preferences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Traditional RLHF trains a reward model from pairwise comparisons and then optimizes the policy using reinforcement learning algorithms such as PPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schulman et al., 2017</a>)</cite>. More recent alternatives like DPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al., 2023</a>)</cite> simplify the pipeline by directly optimizing a policy with preference data, which avoids explicit reward modeling and RL rollouts. In addition, group-based objectives <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>)</cite> have been explored to improve stability and sample efficiency when multiple candidates are available. These approaches provide practical tools for improving calibration, robustness, and controllability, and are increasingly adopted for domain-specific alignment where labeled relevance signals are scarce but preference signals can be collected at scale.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.29897v1/unirank_framework.png" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/179;" width="476" height="179" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1. </span>Overview of <span id="S2.F1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">UniRank</span>. We first perform instruction-driven SFT to obtain a VLM-based reranker. We then mine hard negatives to construct pairwise preferences, train a reward model, and further align the reranker via RLHF for domain-specific hybrid text-image reranking.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3. </span>Multimodal Fusion</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Early progress in visual representation learning was driven by Vision Transformers (ViT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dosovitskiy, 2020</a>)</cite>, which demonstrated that transformer architectures can serve as strong generic backbones for images. Building on such encoders, contrastive vision–language pretraining methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al., 2021</a>)</cite> learned aligned image–text embedding spaces at scale, enabling zero-shot recognition and cross-modal retrieval. Subsequent work further improved vision–language alignment and generation capability via image–text pretraining objectives and multimodal denoising/generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, making it possible to move beyond embedding alignment toward unified multimodal understanding and generation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recent VLMs typically follow a unified architecture that connects a vision encoder to a large language model (LLM) through a modality adapter (e.g., a projection layer or a lightweight Q-Former <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>)</cite>), enabling the LLM to consume visual tokens and perform multimodal reasoning. Representative families include instruction-tuned VLMs such as MiniGPT-4 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, LLaVA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and the Qwen-VL series <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>, which combine large-scale pretraining with instruction tuning to support general-purpose multimodal dialogue, grounding, and reasoning. These advances provide a strong foundation for multimodal IR components, including rerankers, by offering improved cross-modal alignment and flexible interfaces for scoring and decision making over mixed visual and textual evidence.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Methodology</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide a detailed description of <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">UniRank</span>. Specifically, we first formalize the task of reranking over hybrid text-image candidates and clarify the input/output interface of a VLM-based reranker. We then introduce our two-stage domain-specific training pipeline:

<span id="S3.I1" class="ltx_inline-enumerate">
<span id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item">(1)</span> <span id="S3.I1.i1.2" class="ltx_text">supervised fine-tuning with instruction-following data to learn calibrated cross-modal relevance scoring; and
</span></span>
<span id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item">(2)</span> <span id="S3.I1.i2.2" class="ltx_text">preference-based alignment to further improve ranking quality via hard-negative mining, reward model training, and RLHF.
</span></span>
</span>
An overview of the proposed framework is shown in Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Preference alignment and RLHF ‣ 2.2. Instruction Tuning and Preference Alignment ‣ 2. Related Work ‣ UniRank: End-to-End Domain-Specific Reranking of Hybrid text-image Candidates" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Problem Formulation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We study the reranking problem in a hybrid text-image retrieval setting. Given a query <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> and an initial candidate set <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}=\{c_{1},\dots,c_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}=\{c_{1},\dots,c_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math> returned by a first-stage retriever, the goal of reranking is to produce a permutation of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the most relevant candidates appear at the top. In our setting, each query and candidate is a multimodal item that may contain text, images, or both, requiring unified scoring across heterogeneous inputs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A reranker is parameterized by <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> and computes a relevance score for each query–candidate pair:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\theta}(q,c_{i})\in\mathbb{R},\quad c_{i}\in\mathcal{C}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\theta}(q,c_{i})\in\mathbb{R},\quad c_{i}\in\mathcal{C}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The final ranked list is obtained by sorting candidates in descending order of their scores:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi=\mathrm{sort}\big(\mathcal{C};\,s_{\theta}(q,\cdot)\big)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>sort</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi=\mathrm{sort}\big(\mathcal{C};\,s_{\theta}(q,\cdot)\big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the resulting permutation. When ground-truth relevance labels are available, we use <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> to indicate whether <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is relevant to <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>. The training objective is to learn <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> such that relevant candidates receive higher scores than non-relevant ones, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\theta}(q,c^{+})&gt;s_{\theta}(q,c^{-}),\quad c^{+}\in\mathcal{C}^{+},\;c^{-}\in\mathcal{C}^{-}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\theta}(q,c^{+})&gt;s_{\theta}(q,c^{-}),\quad c^{+}\in\mathcal{C}^{+},\;c^{-}\in\mathcal{C}^{-},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> are the relevant and non-relevant subsets of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> for query <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we instantiate <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> using a VLM that natively supports multimodal inputs. Specifically, we represent the query and candidate in their original modalities and feed them into the VLM with an instruction prompt. The VLM then generates a short judgment (e.g., <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">yes</span>/<span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">no</span>) together with token-level likelihoods, from which we derive a scalar relevance score <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\theta}(q,c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\theta}(q,c)</annotation></semantics></math>. This interface enables a single reranker to score text–text, image–image, and cross-modal pairs with a unified scoring function.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Supervised Fine-Tuning</h3>

<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.1. </span>Instruction Data Construction</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To convert a general-purpose VLM into a reranker, we cast reranking as an <em id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">instruction-following judgment</em> task: given a query <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> and a candidate <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, the model is prompted to output a discrete relevance label indicating whether <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> is relevant to <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>. The label space <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> can be domain-specific in practice (e.g., <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">yes</span>/<span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">no</span> for scientific retrieval and <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">high-risk</span>/<span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">low-risk</span> for patent search). Without loss of generality, we denote the two possible outputs as <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}=\{\texttt{yes},\texttt{no}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">yes</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">no</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}=\{\texttt{yes},\texttt{no}\}</annotation></semantics></math> throughout this paper. During inference, we derive a scalar relevance score from the model’s label token logits by applying a sigmoid to the logit difference:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\theta}(q,c)=\sigma\big(\mathrm{logit}_{\theta}(\texttt{yes}\mid q,c)-\mathrm{logit}_{\theta}(\texttt{no}\mid q,c)\big)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>logit</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">yes</mtext><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>logit</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">no</mtext><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\theta}(q,c)=\sigma\big(\mathrm{logit}_{\theta}(\texttt{yes}\mid q,c)-\mathrm{logit}_{\theta}(\texttt{no}\mid q,c)\big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the sigmoid function. This score is then used for standard top-<math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> reranking.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To this end, we serialize each labeled query–candidate pair <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q,c,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q,c,y)</annotation></semantics></math> into a chat-style instruction example, which

<span id="S3.I2" class="ltx_inline-enumerate">
<span id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item">(1)</span> <span id="S3.I2.i1.2" class="ltx_text">supports interleaved text and images and
</span></span>
<span id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item">(2)</span> <span id="S3.I2.i2.2" class="ltx_text">constrains the model to output a single label in <math id="S3.I2.i2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>.
</span></span>
</span>
Specifically, each example is represented as a three-message conversation:</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{m}=\big[m^{\text{sys}},\,m^{\text{usr}},\,m^{\text{asst}}\big]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mtext>sys</mtext></msup><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mtext>usr</mtext></msup><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mtext>asst</mtext></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{m}=\big[m^{\text{sys}},\,m^{\text{usr}},\,m^{\text{asst}}\big],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where

<span id="S3.I3" class="ltx_inline-enumerate">
<span id="S3.I3.i1" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item">(1)</span> <math id="S3.I3.i1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\text{sys}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mtext>sys</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\text{sys}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I3.i1.2" class="ltx_text">specifies the reranking role and restricts the output space to <math id="S3.I3.i1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>;
</span></span>
<span id="S3.I3.i2" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item">(2)</span> <math id="S3.I3.i2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\text{usr}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mtext>usr</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\text{usr}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I3.i2.2" class="ltx_text">provides the query <math id="S3.I3.i2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> and candidate content <math id="S3.I3.i2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> in their native modalities; and
</span></span>
<span id="S3.I3.i3" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item">(3)</span> <math id="S3.I3.i3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\text{asst}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mtext>asst</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\text{asst}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I3.i3.2" class="ltx_text">is the ground-truth label <math id="S3.I3.i3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>.
</span></span>
</span>
Figures <a href="#A4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ SFT and preference data format. ‣ D.1. Scientific Literature Retrieval ‣ Appendix D Prompt Templates and Training Data Examples ‣ UniRank: End-to-End Domain-Specific Reranking of Hybrid text-image Candidates" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and <a href="#A4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ SFT and preference data format. ‣ D.1. Scientific Literature Retrieval ‣ Appendix D Prompt Templates and Training Data Examples ‣ UniRank: End-to-End Domain-Specific Reranking of Hybrid text-image Candidates" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> in Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Prompt Templates and Training Data Examples ‣ UniRank: End-to-End Domain-Specific Reranking of Hybrid text-image Candidates" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a> illustrate the overall prompt structure used in <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">UniRank</span>, including the concrete form of the system instruction and the constrained label output.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To be specific, the system message instructs the model to act as a <em id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">multimodal relevance judge</em> and to respond with <em id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">only one label</em> from <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>, without any explanation. The user message is constructed by concatenating the query and candidate with explicit delimiters:</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="m^{\text{usr}}=\texttt{&lt;QUERY&gt;: }q\;\;\|\;\;\texttt{&lt;DOCUMENT&gt;: }c," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mtext>usr</mtext></msup><mo>=</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">&lt;QUERY&gt;: </mtext><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.560em" rspace="0.727em">∥</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">&lt;DOCUMENT&gt;: </mtext><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\text{usr}}=\texttt{&lt;QUERY&gt;: }q\;\;\|\;\;\texttt{&lt;DOCUMENT&gt;: }c,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∥</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|</annotation></semantics></math> denotes string concatenation. If <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> contains images, we place a special placeholder token <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;image&gt;</span> at the corresponding position in the user message and pass the raw image(s) through the VLM’s vision input channel. This design enables a single prompt template to handle text candidates, image candidates, and mixed query–candidate pairs in a unified manner, without converting one modality into the other. In practice, we construct instruction-following data from in-domain relevance annotations. For each query <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, annotated relevant items form positive pairs <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q,c^{+},y^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q,c^{+},y^{+})</annotation></semantics></math>, while non-relevant items are used to form negative pairs <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q,c^{-},y^{-})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q,c^{-},y^{-})</annotation></semantics></math>. We then serialize each labeled pair <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q,c,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q,c,y)</annotation></semantics></math> into the chat format in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E5" title="In 3.2.1. Instruction Data Construction ‣ 3.2. Supervised Fine-Tuning ‣ 3. Methodology ‣ UniRank: End-to-End Domain-Specific Reranking of Hybrid text-image Candidates" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), producing the final SFT dataset
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{SFT}}=\{\mathbf{m}_{j}\}_{j=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>SFT</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{SFT}}=\{\mathbf{m}_{j}\}_{j=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.2. </span>Supervised Fine-Tuning Objective and Hold-Out Data Split</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given the instruction-following dataset <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{SFT}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>SFT</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{SFT}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we train the VLM reranker by minimizing the standard language modeling loss over the assistant response tokens. Specifically, for each message sequence <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, we compute the negative log-likelihood of the ground-truth label <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> conditioned on the system and user messages:</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(7)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{SFT}}(\theta)=-\log P_{\theta}\big(y\mid m^{\text{sys}},m^{\text{usr}}\big)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>SFT</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mtext>sys</mtext></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mtext>usr</mtext></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{SFT}}(\theta)=-\log P_{\theta}\big(y\mid m^{\text{sys}},m^{\text{usr}}\big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P_{\theta}(y\mid\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\theta}(y\mid\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the model’s conditional probability of generating the label token(s) in <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\text{asst}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mtext>asst</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\text{asst}}</annotation></semantics></math>. In practice, this is implemented as a cross-entropy loss applied only to the output positions corresponding to the label tokens (e.g., <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">yes</span> or <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">no</span>), while masking all other tokens in the sequence. This objective encourages the model to learn calibrated token-level logits that capture domain-specific relevance semantics, which directly underpin the scoring function <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\theta}(q,c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\theta}(q,c)</annotation></semantics></math> defined in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E4" title="In 3.2.1. Instruction Data Construction ‣ 3.2. Supervised Fine-Tuning ‣ 3. Methodology ‣ UniRank: End-to-End Domain-Specific Reranking of Hybrid text-image Candidates" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To support the subsequent preference alignment stage, we reserve a subset of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{SFT}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>SFT</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{SFT}}</annotation></semantics></math> as hold-out data, denoted <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{hold}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>hold</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{hold}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This subset is excluded from SFT optimization and is only used later for constructing pairwise preference data, ensuring that the preference annotations are derived from in-domain examples unseen during SFT, thereby reducing overfitting and improving generalization in the alignment phase.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3. </span>Preference-based Alignment</h3>

<section id="S3.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.1. </span>Preference Data Construction via Hard-Negative Mining</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After SFT, we further improve the reranker via preference-based alignment. This requires a pairwise preference dataset that captures <em id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">in-domain</em> failure modes of the SFT model and provides richer supervision than pointwise labels. To this end, we construct preference data on the held-out split <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{hold}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>hold</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{hold}}</annotation></semantics></math> by mining <em id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">hard</em> query–candidate prompts on which the SFT reranker is most likely to make mistakes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that each query–candidate pair is serialized into a prompt context
<math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}=(m^{\text{sys}},m^{\text{usr}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mtext>sys</mtext></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mtext>usr</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}=(m^{\text{sys}},m^{\text{usr}})</annotation></semantics></math> (Sec. <a href="#S3.SS2.SSS1" title="3.2.1. Instruction Data Construction ‣ 3.2. Supervised Fine-Tuning ‣ 3. Methodology ‣ UniRank: End-to-End Domain-Specific Reranking of Hybrid text-image Candidates" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2.1</span></a>), and the model is required to output a label <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathcal{Y}=\{\texttt{yes},\texttt{no}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">yes</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">no</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathcal{Y}=\{\texttt{yes},\texttt{no}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For each query <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in\mathcal{D}_{\text{hold}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>hold</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in\mathcal{D}_{\text{hold}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we score all candidates <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in\mathcal{C}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in\mathcal{C}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> using the SFT model and rank them by <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\theta}(q,c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\theta}(q,c)</annotation></semantics></math>. We then focus on the top-<math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> candidates and select <em id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">hard</em> prompts where the model tends to over-estimate relevance, i.e., non-relevant candidates appearing in the top-<math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(8)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{q}=\big\{c\in\mathrm{top}\text{-}N(\mathcal{C}_{q})\;\big|\;y(q,c)=0\big\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>top</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="1.200em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{q}=\big\{c\in\mathrm{top}\text{-}N(\mathcal{C}_{q})\;\big|\;y(q,c)=0\big\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y(q,c)\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y(q,c)\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the ground-truth relevance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For each hard negative candidate <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{-}\in\mathcal{H}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{-}\in\mathcal{H}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, we create a pairwise preference instance at the <em id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">response</em> level by keeping the same prompt <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}=(m^{\text{sys}},m^{\text{usr}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mtext>sys</mtext></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mtext>usr</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}=(m^{\text{sys}},m^{\text{usr}})</annotation></semantics></math> and contrasting the <em id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">correct</em> label <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> against the <em id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">incorrect</em> one <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{y}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(9)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\big(\mathbf{x},\,y^{\star},\,\bar{y}\big),\quad y^{\star}=\texttt{no},\;\;\bar{y}=\texttt{yes}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">no</mtext></mrow><mo rspace="0.727em">,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">yes</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\big(\mathbf{x},\,y^{\star},\,\bar{y}\big),\quad y^{\star}=\texttt{no},\;\;\bar{y}=\texttt{yes}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">That is, for hard negatives the preferred response is the ground-truth label (<span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">no</span>) and the rejected response is the SFT model’s typical error mode (<span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">yes</span>). Symmetrically, for positive prompts <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q,c^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q,c^{+})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y(q,c^{+})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y(q,c^{+})=1</annotation></semantics></math>, we construct</p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(10)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\big(\mathbf{x},\,y^{\star},\,\bar{y}\big),\quad y^{\star}=\texttt{yes},\;\;\bar{y}=\texttt{no}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">yes</mtext></mrow><mo rspace="0.727em">,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">no</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\big(\mathbf{x},\,y^{\star},\,\bar{y}\big),\quad y^{\star}=\texttt{yes},\;\;\bar{y}=\texttt{no}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Aggregating over queries yields the final preference dataset <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{pref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>pref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{pref}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This construction directly targets label-level confusions on difficult in-domain prompts, and is well matched to our reranker formulation where relevance is inferred from label token likelihoods.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.2. </span>Reward Model Training</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given the preference dataset <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{pref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>pref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{pref}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we train a reward model to predict which <em id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">response</em> is preferred under the same prompt. Concretely, the RM shares the same multimodal prompting interface as the reranker. Let <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}=(m^{\text{sys}},m^{\text{usr}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mtext>sys</mtext></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mtext>usr</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}=(m^{\text{sys}},m^{\text{usr}})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the prompt context constructed from a query–candidate pair, and let <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> be a label token sequence. The RM takes <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{x},y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{x},y)</annotation></semantics></math> as input and outputs a scalar reward</p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(11)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\phi}(\mathbf{x},y)\in\mathbb{R}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\phi}(\mathbf{x},y)\in\mathbb{R},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the RM parameters. Intuitively, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\phi}(\mathbf{x},y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\phi}(\mathbf{x},y)</annotation></semantics></math> measures how appropriate it is to respond with label <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> under prompt <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, and thus reflects in-domain relevance preferences.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We optimize the RM using a standard pairwise preference loss. For each preference instance <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{x},y^{\star},\bar{y})\in\mathcal{D}_{\text{pref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>pref</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{x},y^{\star},\bar{y})\in\mathcal{D}_{\text{pref}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> is the preferred (chosen) label and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{y}</annotation></semantics></math> is the rejected label, we minimize:</p>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(12)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{RM}}(\phi)=-\mathbb{E}_{(\mathbf{x},y^{\star},\bar{y})\sim\mathcal{D}_{\text{pref}}}\Big[\log\sigma\big(r_{\phi}(\mathbf{x},y^{\star})-r_{\phi}(\mathbf{x},\bar{y})\big)\Big]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>pref</mtext></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{RM}}(\phi)=-\mathbb{E}_{(\mathbf{x},y^{\star},\bar{y})\sim\mathcal{D}_{\text{pref}}}\Big[\log\sigma\big(r_{\phi}(\mathbf{x},y^{\star})-r_{\phi}(\mathbf{x},\bar{y})\big)\Big],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the sigmoid function. This loss corresponds to a Bradley–Terry style model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bradley and Terry, 1952</a>)</cite> of pairwise preferences and is widely used in RLHF for learning reward functions from comparison data. Minimizing <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{RM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{RM}}</annotation></semantics></math> encourages the RM to assign higher reward to the preferred label than to the rejected one under the same prompt.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In practice, we initialize the RM from the SFT reranker to preserve domain knowledge and multimodal alignment, and then fine-tune it on <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{pref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>pref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{pref}}</annotation></semantics></math> using the above objective.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.3. </span>RLHF Training</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With the learned reward model, we further align the reranker via RLHF to directly optimize ranking behavior under in-domain preferences. We initialize the policy from the SFT reranker, i.e., <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}\leftarrow\pi_{\theta_{\text{SFT}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>SFT</mtext></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}\leftarrow\pi_{\theta_{\text{SFT}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and treat the reranker as a policy that, given a prompt context <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}=(m^{\text{sys}},m^{\text{usr}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mtext>sys</mtext></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mtext>usr</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}=(m^{\text{sys}},m^{\text{usr}})</annotation></semantics></math>, generates a label <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>. The reward model <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\phi}(\mathbf{x},y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\phi}(\mathbf{x},y)</annotation></semantics></math> provides a learned training signal to encourage better calibrated and more robust relevance scoring.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">UniRank</span> is compatible with a range of preference-optimization objectives and RLHF algorithms. In our experiments, we adopt <em id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Group Relative Policy Optimization</em> (GRPO) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. A key design choice is that we form <em id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">query-level groups</em> rather than the standard prompt-level groups used in typical GRPO settings. That is, instead of sampling multiple responses for a single prompt, we exploit the inherent listwise structure of reranking and compare multiple <em id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">candidate prompts</em> that share the same query.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We construct groups directly from the preference data <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{pref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>pref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{pref}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which already contains prompt contexts derived from held-out in-domain queries. Let <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the set of prompt contexts in <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{pref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>pref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{pref}}</annotation></semantics></math> associated with query <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> (each corresponding to a distinct candidate under <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>). For each query-level group <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{x}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{x}_{G}\}\subseteq\mathcal{X}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{x}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{x}_{G}\}\subseteq\mathcal{X}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, the current policy generates labels <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="y_{i}\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid\mathbf{x}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid\mathbf{x}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> and receives rewards</p>
<table id="S3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(13)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="r_{i}=r_{\phi}(\mathbf{x}_{i},y_{i}),\quad i=1,\ldots,G." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}=r_{\phi}(\mathbf{x}_{i},y_{i}),\quad i=1,\ldots,G.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">We normalize rewards within the group to obtain relative advantages:</p>
<table id="S3.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(14)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}=\frac{r_{i}-\mu_{r}}{\sigma_{r}+\delta},\quad\mu_{r}=\frac{1}{G}\sum_{j=1}^{G}r_{j},\quad\sigma_{r}=\sqrt{\frac{1}{G}\sum_{j=1}^{G}(r_{j}-\mu_{r})^{2}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>G</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>G</mi></munderover><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>G</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>G</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}=\frac{r_{i}-\mu_{r}}{\sigma_{r}+\delta},\quad\mu_{r}=\frac{1}{G}\sum_{j=1}^{G}r_{j},\quad\sigma_{r}=\sqrt{\frac{1}{G}\sum_{j=1}^{G}(r_{j}-\mu_{r})^{2}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p3.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> is a small constant for numerical stability. The policy is then updated using a clipped surrogate objective:</p>
<table id="S3.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(15)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{GRPO}}(\theta)=-\mathbb{E}\Bigg[\frac{1}{G}\sum_{i=1}^{G}\min\Big(\rho_{i}(\theta)\,A_{i},\;\mathrm{clip}(\rho_{i}(\theta),1-\epsilon,1+\epsilon)\,A_{i}\Big)\Bigg]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>GRPO</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">[</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>G</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>G</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>clip</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{GRPO}}(\theta)=-\mathbb{E}\Bigg[\frac{1}{G}\sum_{i=1}^{G}\min\Big(\rho_{i}(\theta)\,A_{i},\;\mathrm{clip}(\rho_{i}(\theta),1-\epsilon,1+\epsilon)\,A_{i}\Big)\Bigg],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p3.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{i}(\theta)=\frac{\pi_{\theta}(y_{i}\mid\mathbf{x}_{i})}{\pi_{\theta_{\text{ref}}}(y_{i}\mid\mathbf{x}_{i})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{i}(\theta)=\frac{\pi_{\theta}(y_{i}\mid\mathbf{x}_{i})}{\pi_{\theta_{\text{ref}}}(y_{i}\mid\mathbf{x}_{i})}</annotation></semantics></math> is the policy ratio, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is the clipping hyperparameter, and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta_{\text{ref}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta_{\text{ref}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a fixed reference policy (set to <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta_{\text{SFT}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>SFT</mtext></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta_{\text{SFT}}}</annotation></semantics></math>) to regularize updates. By contrasting candidates <em id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p3.4.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">within the same query</em>, <span id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p3.4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">UniRank</span> directly optimizes the relative ordering behavior that reranking requires, thereby improving robustness on hard in-domain cases.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">After RLHF optimization, we use the aligned policy to compute the final relevance score <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\theta}(q,c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\theta}(q,c)</annotation></semantics></math> for reranking.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we evaluate <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">UniRank</span> on two domain-specific multimodal retrieval tasks, including scientific literature retrieval and design patent search, where the reranking candidate set contains <em id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">hybrid</em> text-image items. Our evaluation is designed to answer the following research questions:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ1 (Effectiveness in domain-specific hybrid reranking).</span>
Does <span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">UniRank</span> achieve better reranking performance than strong text-only and VLM-based multimodal baselines in domain-specific settings with hybrid text-image candidates?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ2 (Robustness across retrieval setups and model scales).</span>
How robust is <span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">UniRank</span> under different first-stage retrievers (which change the candidate distribution) and different VLM backbone sizes (which change capacity)?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ3 (Contribution of each training component).</span>
What is the contribution of each component in <span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">UniRank</span>’s end-to-end training pipeline to the final reranking quality?</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Setup</h3>

<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Datasets.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">UniRank</span> on two domain-specific multimodal retrieval tasks with hybrid text-image candidate sets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scientific literature retrieval.</span>
We evaluate on the <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">MMDocIR</span> benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dong et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and adopt its <em id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Academic Paper</em> domain under the <em id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">layout-retrieval</em> setting.
Each document is a formal academic publication consisting of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> layout regions (e.g., paragraphs, figures, and tables) with structured layouts, citations, and academic visuals.
Given a natural-language question <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> (text-only), the system retrieves and reranks the top-<math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> most relevant layout regions from the paper.
This task naturally yields a heterogeneous candidate pool spanning multiple modalities: approximately 28.8% text regions, 25.7% image regions, and 50.0% table regions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Design patent search.</span>
We additionally evaluate on a real-world design patent search task for product appearance risk screening.
Given a <em id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">target product</em> as the query, the goal is to score each <em id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">comparison patent</em> by whether it is <em id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">substantially similar</em> in overall visual impression, and rerank the recalled patents accordingly.
Both queries and candidates are multimodal: a product includes a main image with optional title/description, while a patent contains structured fields such as patent title, representative figure, abstract, specification text, and metadata.
We construct the dataset by first retrieving candidate patents using an off-the-shelf appearance-based retrieval system, and then assigning binary labels (<span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">high-risk</span>/<span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">low-risk</span>) using <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">gemini-3-pro-preview</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, which we treat as supervision for training and evaluation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Dataset statistics for both domains, including the splits used for supervised fine-tuning (<math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{SFT}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>SFT</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{SFT}}</annotation></semantics></math>), hold-out preference construction (<math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{hold}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>hold</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{hold}}</annotation></semantics></math>), and the test set (<math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{test}}</annotation></semantics></math>), are summarized in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Datasets. ‣ 4.1. Setup ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ UniRank: End-to-End Domain-Specific Reranking of Hybrid text-image Candidates" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
Additional qualitative examples and details of dataset construction and labeling are provided in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Dataset Construction Details ‣ UniRank: End-to-End Domain-Specific Reranking of Hybrid text-image Candidates" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1. </span>Statistics of the two domain-specific datasets used in our experiments. All splits are disjoint.
</figcaption>
<table id="S4.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Domain</span></th>
<th id="S4.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Split</span></th>
<th id="S4.T1.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T1.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"># Docs</span></th>
<th id="S4.T1.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T1.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"># Queries</span></th>
<th id="S4.T1.2.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T1.2.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"># QA Pairs</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T1.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" rowspan="3"><span id="S4.T1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Scientific</span></th>
<th id="S4.T1.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t"><math id="S4.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{SFT}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>SFT</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{SFT}}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S4.T1.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t">957</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t">3980</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t">71821</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row"><math id="S4.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{hold}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>hold</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{hold}}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S4.T1.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">239</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">995</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">17955</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row"><math id="S4.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{test}}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S4.T1.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">75</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">386</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">6840</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" rowspan="3"><span id="S4.T1.2.5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Patent</span></th>
